# The LIGHT gate, `complete:light`. It keeps every static check (lint, format,
# generated-types freshness, spec identity, docs references, `vue-tsc`) plus the unit suite.
# The backend lints the contract; the frontend only verifies its copy is identical.
#
# Left out (CI on push runs all of it). A lane merges on `complete:light` plus every Cypress spec it
# adds or changes, run by file; the full `npm run complete` runs on main at the checkpoints:
#
# - the Vite build and Cypress e2e against the demo dataset — most of the full gate's minutes;
#
# `test:e2e:live` is NOT here, and it is the gate that actually proves behaviour — it needs the
# paired backend, a Mongo and a Redis, so it runs in CI as a required job instead. A green commit
# hook is not a claim that this frontend agrees with that backend; the PR is.
#
# The spec-identity check SKIPS rather than fails when the paired backend is not on disk, so a
# half-cloned pair can still commit. It stays fatal under CI, where a missing sibling means the
# workflow is misconfigured rather than absent.
#
# Deliberately not here, and run by hand for the occasions that want them: `test:mutation`
# (re-executes the unit suite once per mutant — nightly in CI), and `complete:manual`, which is
# `test:e2e:live` (needs the paired backend up). Pixel diffs answer to the machine that took them,
# so they run only in the pinned image of `visual.yml`.
#
# Lint and format check the STAGED SNAPSHOT, not the working tree. `lint-staged`
# (`.lintstagedrc.json`) runs `eslint` and `prettier --check` on staged files only. Over the whole
# tree, a peer's unrelated dirty file — or a scratch note sitting in the same checkout — could fail
# a commit that never touched it, and a `prettier --write` would rewrite every unformatted file in
# the tree, not only the ones being committed. `type-check-only` and `test:unit` below stay
# whole-project on purpose:
# `vue-tsc` needs the whole project graph regardless of what changed, and neither one can be tricked
# into failing by an unrelated dirty file the way a tree-wide format check can.
npx lint-staged

# The generated contract code is gitignored and rebuilt from `openapi.yaml` / `asyncapi.yaml`, so
# regenerating it is only needed when one of those two is staged: the checks below read the result.
# Nothing here stages anything.
if git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR | grep -qE '^(openapi|asyncapi)\.yaml$'; then
    npm run regenerate
fi

npm run complete:light:gates
